        "thinking": "So `this.ctx.container` is `Container | undefined` on the `DurableObjectState`. This means a regular Durable Object class can optionally have a container associated with it.\n\nThe proper pattern is:\n1. Create a DO class that uses `this.ctx.container` to communicate with a Docker container\n2. Declare it in `[[containers]]` with `class_name` AND `[[durable_objects.bindings]]` with `name` and `class_name`\n\nThe `[[containers]]` section doesn't use `binding` — it's linked to the DO class via `class_name`. The actual binding name is in `[[durable_objects.bindings]]`.\n\n\nNow I need to implement this fix: update wrangler.toml to remove the incorrect `binding` from containers and add proper `class_name` references, create two Durable Object classes that proxy requests to their respective containers on port 8080, and update the environment types to use DurableObjectNamespace instead of Fetcher for the container services.\n\nThen I'll update the heartbeat and plugin dispatch logic to use the Durable Object stubs instead of direct fetches, which means the requests will route through the DO's fetch handler that proxies them to the actual container service.\n\nFor local development, I need to handle the case where `this.ctx.container` is undefined since Docker won't be running during `wrangler dev`, so the fetch handler should gracefully handle that scenario.\n\nThe issue is that `SIDECAR_SERVICE` will be present as a binding even locally, so I can't use its presence to detect whether containers are actually available—I need either a separate environment flag to control the fallback behavior, or have the SidecarContainer DO itself attempt the container fetch and fall back if it fails.\n\nThe cleanest solution is having the DO check if `this.ctx.container` exists; if it does, it starts the container and proxies requests to it, but if not (local dev), it reads the KV bridge URL from its environment bindings and proxies through that instead.\n\nWriting the DO fetch handler... Writing the PluginContainer class... I need to reconsider the type setup here. If I'm using `DurableObjectNamespace` for the bindings, then all the fetch calls throughout heartbeat.ts and plugin-dispatch.ts need to switch to the `idFromName()` pattern instead of calling fetch directly. Let me trace through and update those files consistently with the proper Durable Object stub pattern. I'm realizing that using a fixed name like \"sidecar\" means all requests route to the same Durable Object instance, which works fine for a singleton pattern, but for plugin containers I could use the job ID as the name to create separate instances for parallel execution—though I'll keep it simple with a single \"plugin-container\" instance for now. Before I start writing the implementation, I need to verify how DurableObjectNamespace behaves in local wrangler dev.\n\nFor local development, the DO bindings should be resolvable, and when the container's fetch method runs, `this.ctx.container` will be undefined, so it'll correctly fall back to the bridge URL from KV. In production with CF Containers, it'll proxy directly to the Docker container instead.",
